\chapter{Who Gets to Call It Unix?}
\label{ch:wars}
\index{Unix wars}\index{POSIX}\index{Single UNIX Specification}\index{licensing}

\section{A successful family becomes a commercial argument}

By the 1980s, Unix was simultaneously a research tradition, licensed source
code, a commercial product family, and a valuable name. These meanings
could pull in different directions. Researchers wanted to revise a system.
Application vendors wanted stable interfaces. Hardware vendors wanted to
differentiate their machines while attracting the same software.
The owner of the Unix intellectual property wanted a viable business.

The Bell System's divestiture, effective in 1984, changed AT\&T's commercial
setting. Unix development associated with AT\&T's product organization
became increasingly important alongside the research editions.
System III and System V were not simply new names for the next research
manuals. They belonged to a product line that integrated work from several
sources and served customers with different expectations.

The familiar contrast between ``BSD'' and ``System V'' was consequently
both useful and incomplete. Systems differed in terminal interfaces,
interprocess communication, administration, libraries, and networking.
Yet vendors borrowed features, offered compatibility facilities, and
assembled hybrids. A real installed system often resisted classification
as one pure branch \parencite[ch. 7--8]{kernighan-history}.

For application developers, these differences imposed repeated work.
Source could be portable in principle while requiring conditional code,
separate build configurations, and extensive testing. Customers could
pay the cost indirectly through delayed releases and limited support.
The pressure for standardization was therefore practical as well as political.

\section{Two kinds of openness compete}
\index{Open Software Foundation}\index{Unix International}\index{System V Release 4}

AT\&T and Sun's cooperation on a converged Unix intensified concern among
other vendors about who would control the platform. The Open Software
Foundation, formed in 1988 by a group of major companies, offered one
institutional answer. Unix International supported the AT\&T-oriented
alternative. ``Open'' did not yet guarantee the freedoms later associated
with an open source license. It could mean vendor independence,
standard interfaces, consortium membership, or access under commercial terms.

System V Release 4 combined important elements from System V, BSD,
SunOS, and Xenix. The act of integration was technically meaningful even
though it did not end the market's disagreements. A converged source base
could still produce distinct products, and a common specification could
still leave room for incompatible extensions.

The Unix wars were not one battle with a clear end date. They included
alliances, branding, standards work, desktop competition, procurement,
and the positioning of proprietary products. Some efforts reduced
incompatibility; others multiplied the institutions claiming to solve it.
The desire for a single market sometimes produced more standards before
it produced fewer.

It is tempting to declare that the fighting alone caused Unix vendors to
lose the desktop. That explanation is too tidy. Hardware economics,
application availability, user experience, Microsoft's growth, and the
emergence of inexpensive Unix-like systems all mattered. Standards conflict
was part of the cost structure, not a sufficient explanation for every
commercial outcome.

\section{What POSIX can promise}

A standard can define an interface without prescribing its implementation.
POSIX specifies operating-system services and, in its broader body of
specifications, a shell and utilities environment. A conforming program
can rely on documented behavior within that scope. The first IEEE
1003.1 standard appeared in 1988; later revisions expanded and consolidated
the specification.

This is source-level agreement, not an instruction-set translator.
A C program using specified interfaces may be recompiled for another
conforming system. A binary still depends on processor architecture,
calling conventions, executable format, dynamic linking, and other ABI
properties. The distinction between API and ABI explains why two systems
can both implement POSIX without running each other's executables
\parencite[ch. 2]{apue}.

A specification also has boundaries. It may mark a feature optional,
leave a value implementation-defined, or permit several outcomes.
A program that accidentally relies on an unspecified behavior can work
on one system and fail on another without either system violating the
standard. Portability requires reading those qualifications rather than
merely recognizing a familiar function name.

Standards do not freeze all development. A system can provide extensions
outside the common contract. Those extensions may solve important
problems, such as scalable event notification or specialized storage
control. The author must decide whether the benefit justifies limiting
portability. The useful question is not whether extensions are morally
wrong, but whether the dependency is deliberate and documented.

\section{A trademark is a different kind of interface}
\index{UNIX certification}\index{The Open Group}

The Single UNIX Specification and certification associated with The Open
Group give the UNIX name a formal meaning beyond family resemblance.
Certification applies to a product and version in a defined environment.
It should not be inferred from a kernel's ancestry or from a distributor's
claim to be Unix-like.

This makes several apparently contradictory statements compatible.
A system can be recognizably Unix in design without being certified.
A Linux-based product can pursue or obtain certification without acquiring
AT\&T kernel code. A system descended from historical Unix does not
automatically certify every future release or hardware configuration.
Genealogy, interface conformance, and trademark permission are three
separate axes.

Keeping those axes separate is especially helpful for Android and macOS.
Android uses Linux but does not present a conventional desktop Unix
application environment. Apple's product history includes certified UNIX
environments, but that does not make every Apple operating system or every
version interchangeable. The contemporary landscape becomes clearer when
names are treated as claims with a scope.

\section{The BSD legal transition}
\index{USL litigation}\index{4.4BSD-Lite}\index{Bostic, Keith}

Berkeley developers worked to replace code that required an AT\&T license.
Keith Bostic's effort to obtain replacement utilities and the work of the
CSRG on the kernel made progressively freer distributions possible.
Networking Release 1 and Networking Release 2 were milestones in this
process. They should not be mistaken for the first day anyone shared BSD
software, nor should Net/2 be described as the final legal resolution.

Unix System Laboratories brought litigation involving Berkeley Software
Design, Inc., and the University of California in 1992. Claims,
counterclaims, and the resulting uncertainty affected the BSD ecosystem.
The 1994 settlement and the release of 4.4BSD-Lite provided an important
basis for further development without the old AT\&T source-license
requirement. The legal outcome was more specific than either ``all Unix
became free'' or ``Berkeley proved every claim false''
\parencite[ch. 13]{salus}.

The timing overlapped with Linux's early growth, but overlap does not prove
a single cause. Linux also benefited from inexpensive PCs, accessible
collaboration, existing GNU tools, and a growing distribution ecosystem.
The litigation was one influence on people's choices; it was not an
experiment that isolated the cause of Linux adoption.

FreeBSD and NetBSD began in 1993, OpenBSD branched from NetBSD in 1995,
and DragonFly BSD later branched from FreeBSD. Their communities established
different priorities and release practices. The shared BSD label does not
mean that they are editions of one centrally managed product. Their
histories show how legal independence can support technical diversity
rather than force convergence.

\section{Licenses shape future choices}

A permissive license typically allows redistribution, modification, and
incorporation into larger products subject to conditions such as retaining
notices. A copyleft license adds conditions designed to preserve specified
freedoms when covered work is conveyed. The exact obligations depend on
the actual license and circumstances, not on the casual labels ``BSD'' or
``GPL.''

In particular, the GPL does not generally require every private modification
to be published to the world. Distribution and corresponding-source
obligations must be understood in context. Nor does a permissive license
mean that authors have abandoned copyright or attribution. Both approaches
use legal permissions to structure collaboration; they select different
rules for downstream behavior.

This is why licensing belongs inside a technical history. A kernel may be
elegant but unavailable for a needed use. A modest tool with clear
redistribution rights can become infrastructure for products its authors
never anticipated. An interface tells programmers how components can
connect; a license helps determine who may make and share those connections.

The Unix wars showed how much economic value accumulated around such
agreements. GNU, Linux, and the freely redistributable BSD systems would
change the bargaining position. A user could increasingly choose a Unix-like
environment whose right to continue developing did not depend on one
proprietor's continued cooperation.
